      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "In asthma, persistent inflammation might be the result of (1) an impaired ability to clear inflammatory cells from the airways and/or (2) impaired apoptotic responses."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "In a mouse model, we investigated the regulatory role of Fas (CD95)-induced apoptosis in the development and resolution of airway inflammation and airway hyperresponsiveness (AHR)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "Mice that were either Fas-sufficient (wild-type; WT) or Fas-deficient (lpr ) were sensitized by intraperitoneal injections of ovalbumin (OVA) and challenged once intranasally with OVA (IP-IN mice). Control (IN) mice were challenged only."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "IP-IN WT mice developed AHR at 48 hours; changes in airway resistance resolved by 96 hours. Airway responsiveness at 48 hours in IP-IN lpr mice was similar to that in IP-IN WT mice. However, in contrast to WT mice, IP-IN lpr mice sustained significant AHR at 96 hours in comparison with IN lpr mice; the AHR resolved by 6 days. Bronchoalveolar lavage fluid cell composition was similar in all of the different groups at 48 hours and 96 hours. Both IP-IN WT mice and lpr mice exhibited similar tissue eosinophilia, whereas IP-IN lpr mice had significantly lower numbers of TdT-mediated dUTP nick end labeling (TUNEL)-positive cells in comparison with IP-IN WT mice at 48 hours. Anti-IL-5 antibody given to IP-IN lpr mice 48 hours and 72 hours after the challenge significantly decreased AHR and eosinophilic inflammation and increased TUNEL-positive cell numbers at 96 hours."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "These results suggest that Fas expression can regulate the onset and resolution of AHR through an increase in eosinophil apoptosis."
        }
      ]},
